  "response": "```python\ndef reverse_list_elements(elements, punctuation):\n    \"\"\"\n    Reverses the order of elements and punctuation marks while maintaining their positional association.\n\n    Args:\n        elements: List of strings representing elements\n        punctuation: List of punctuation marks (strings) corresponding to each element\n\n    Returns:\n        Tuple containing (reversed_elements, reversed_punctuation)\n    \"\"\"\n    reversed_elements = list(reversed(elements))\n    reversed_punctuation = list(reversed(punctuation))\n    return (reversed_elements, reversed_punctuation)\n\n\n# Alternative implementation without using reversed()\ndef reverse_list_elements(elements, punctuation):\n    if len(elements) != len(punctuation):\n        raise ValueError(\"Elements and punctuation lists must be the same length\")\n    \n    # Reverse both lists\n    new_elements = elements[::-1]\n    new_punctuation = punctuation[::-1]\n    \n    return (new_elements, new_punctuation)\n\n\n# Example usage:\nif __name__ == \"__main__\":\n    # Example 1 from problem\n    result = reverse_list_elements([\"Hello\", \"world\"], [\"\", \"!\"])\n    print(f\"Example 1: {result}\")\n    # Output: (['world', 'Hello'], ['!', ''])\n    \n    # Example 2: Elements with punctuation\n    elements2 = [\"Hi!\", \"How?\", \"Yes.\" \"No?\"]\n    punctuation2 = [! ? . , \"]\n    result2 = reverse_list_elements(elements2, punctuation2)\n    print(f\"Example 2: {result2}\")\n```\n\n**Notes:**\n\n- The function maintains a one-to-one positional association between elements and punctuation marks. When reversing the list, both corresponding entries are reversed together to preserve their pairing.\n\n- The first implementation uses Python's built-in `reversed()` function for readability, while the second uses slicing (`[::-1]`) for performance in large datasets.\n\n- Input validation ensures that `elements` and `punctuation` lists are of equal length. Adjust punctuation values accordingly—e.g., `\"Hi!\"` may or may not include punctuation depending on your use case.",
